# Target C-II — terminal FREE surplus-endpoint stability and pentagonal-flower normal form

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proved candidate awaiting audit; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II terminal FREE survivor sequences with `pi D-q=o(D)`  
**Depends on:** scope-safe matched all-train gap `g=0.007707`; exact post-endpoint surplus decomposition; terminal stopped-train/face identities; exact singleton/full-flower incidence lemma  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_surplus_endpoint_stability_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

This note keeps a currency that the current terminal FREE package has so far discarded:
the all-phase surplus

\[
\mu_D:=\frac{e_{BB}+2s_C-3(d_H+s_Z)}D.
\]

The exact post-endpoint decomposition and the matched all-train gap imply a new
terminal floor

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\mu_D\ge
\mu_*:=1.953262849540479\ldots .
}
\tag{SE.1}
\]

The numerical value happens to equal the current terminal feasibility floor for
`t=(q-k)/D`, but the surplus identity gives substantially more information than that
scalar coincidence.  If `mu_D` is within `epsilon` of (SE.1), then the actual outer
walk is nearly geodesic, the superpentagonal degree excess is small, the force-hull
nonunit-side currency is small, and the train slack is near its matched minimum.  In
particular, almost `0.765D` bounded contact faces are pentagons at the exact endpoint.

The endpoint structure then admits an exact singleton-versus-fully-rhombic-flower
split.  At `epsilon=0`, either at least

\[
0.254977681830389\ldots D
\]

singleton stopped trains survive, or at least that many bounded pentagons have a
strict rhombus on every one of their five sides.  In the flower branch at least

\[
0.637444204575972\ldots D
\]

distinct non-singleton rhombic trains terminate at those pentagons and the rhombic
train Mantel deficit is at least

\[
(1.698340455132259\ldots-o(1))D^2.
\]

No source-level closure is claimed.  The gain is an endpoint rigidity theorem for a
currency (`mu_D`) already central to the canonical full-core route.

---

## 1. Constants and exact decomposition

Put

\[
s_2:=\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}},
\qquad g:=0.007707,
\]

\[
\tau_*:=\frac\pi2+g,
\qquad
M_*:=4s_2+2g
=3.824665227455829\ldots .
\tag{SE.2}
\]

The matched terminal FREE package gives

\[
\frac{\tau_D}{D}\ge\tau_*-o(1),
\qquad
\tau_D:=T-2\sqrt{R_4}.
\tag{SE.3}
\]

Use the exact stopped-train split

\[
2\tau_D=\rho_{\rm rec}+\sigma_R.
\tag{SE.4}
\]

The exact post-endpoint surplus identity is

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mu_DD={}&{2P_F+2\sqrt{R_4}\over5}
+m_B+{2\over5}(Q-f)+{2\over5}(b-P_F)\\
&+{1\over10}\rho_{\rm rec}+{1\over10}\sigma_R-2c_I-4.
\end{aligned}
\tag{SE.5}
\]

Here `m_B>=0`, `Q-f>=0`, and `b-P_F>=0`; canonical component control gives
`c_I=o(D)`.  Terminal Type II gives

\[
\frac{P_F}{D}\to\pi,
\qquad
\frac{\sqrt{R_4}}D\to s_2.
\tag{SE.6}
\]

Combining (SE.3)--(SE.6),

\[
\liminf\mu_D
\ge
{2\pi+2s_2\over5}+{\tau_*\over5}
=\frac\pi2+\frac{2s_2}{5}+\frac g5.
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\mu_*
={2\pi+2s_2\over5}+{\pi/2+g\over5}
=1.953262849540479\ldots .
}
\tag{SE.7}
\]

This proves (SE.1).

---

## 2. Quantitative stability above the surplus endpoint

Fix `epsilon>=0` and pass to a subsequence on which all normalized currencies used
below converge.  Assume

\[
\limsup\mu_D\le\mu_*+\varepsilon.
\tag{SE.8}
\]

Write

\[
\delta:=\lim\frac{b-P_F}{D},
\qquad
r:=\lim\frac{Q-f}{D},
\qquad
m:=\lim\frac{m_B}{D},
\]

and

\[
a:=\lim\left(\frac{\tau_D}{D}-\tau_*\right).
\]

After a further subsequence if needed, (SE.3) gives `a>=0`.  Subtracting the endpoint
value (SE.7) from (SE.5) gives the exact asymptotic nonnegative decomposition

\[
\boxed{
 m+\frac25(r+\delta)+\frac15a\le\varepsilon.
}
\tag{SE.9}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
 m\le\varepsilon,
\qquad
r+\delta\le\frac52\varepsilon,
\qquad
a\le5\varepsilon.
}
\tag{SE.10}
\]

Thus surplus near-equality simultaneously forces:

- near-geodesic outer walk: `delta<=5 epsilon/2`;
- almost-pure pentagonal exceptional degree: `r<=5 epsilon/2`;
- vanishing nonunit force-hull-side currency: `m<=epsilon`;
- train slack within `5 epsilon` of the matched lower edge.

At `epsilon=0`, all four quantities vanish.

---

## 3. Near the endpoint there are about `0.765D` pentagons

Let

\[
B:=\lim\frac{Q+4f}{D}.
\]

The exact endpoint handshake is

\[
(b-P_F)+(Q+4f)=2T-P_F.
\tag{SE.11}
\]

Using `T=\tau_D+2\sqrt{R_4}` and (SE.6),

\[
\boxed{
B+\delta=M_*+2a.
}
\tag{SE.12}
\]

Since `Q=f+(Q-f)`, one also has

\[
B=5\frac fD+r.
\tag{SE.13}
\]

Therefore every subsequential limit satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\frac fD\to {M_*+2a-\delta-r\over5},
\qquad
\frac QD\to {M_*+2a-\delta+4r\over5}.
}
\tag{SE.14}
\]

Equations (SE.10) and (SE.14) imply the stability interval

\[
\boxed{
{M_*\over5}-{\varepsilon\over2}
\le\liminf {f\over D}
\le\limsup {f\over D}
\le {M_*\over5}+2\varepsilon,
}
\tag{SE.15}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
{M_*\over5}-{\varepsilon\over2}
\le\liminf {Q\over D}
\le\limsup {Q\over D}
\le {M_*\over5}+4\varepsilon.
}
\tag{SE.16}
\]

Let `P_5` be the number of bounded pentagonal contact faces.  Degree arithmetic gives

\[
P_5\ge2f-Q=f-(Q-f).
\tag{SE.17}
\]

Combining (SE.10), (SE.14), and (SE.17),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {P_5\over D}
\ge {M_*\over5}-3\varepsilon.
}
\tag{SE.18}
\]

Also the number of bounded exceptional faces of degree at least six is at most
`Q-f`, hence at most `(5/2)epsilon D+o(D)`.

At exact surplus saturation,

\[
\boxed{
\frac fD,\frac QD,\frac{P_5}D
\longrightarrow
{M_*\over5}
=0.764933045491166\ldots .
}
\tag{SE.19}
\]

Thus the endpoint is not the earlier balanced `0.478D` pentagon regime.  It is an
outer-geodesic, asymptotically all-pentagonal exceptional network with roughly
`0.765D` bounded pentagons.

---

## 4. Singleton versus fully rhombic pentagonal flowers

Call a bounded pentagon fully rhombic if each of its five sides has a strict unit
rhombus on the opposite face side, and let `F_5^{full}` be their number.  Let `S` be
the singleton stopped-train count.

The exact finite incidence inequality already proved for the modern train ledger is

\[
\boxed{P_5-F_5^{full}\le2S.}
\tag{SE.20}
\]

Indeed every non-full pentagon has a singleton boundary edge, while a singleton edge
can witness at most two pentagons.

Set

\[
p_\varepsilon:={M_*\over5}-3\varepsilon.
\]

Whenever `p_epsilon>0`, (SE.18) and (SE.20) give

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{\liminf{S\over D},
\liminf{F_5^{full}\over D}\right\}
\ge {p_\varepsilon\over3}
={M_*\over15}-\varepsilon.
}
\tag{SE.21}
\]

More precisely, along every fixed subsequence either

\[
\frac SD\ge {M_*\over15}-\varepsilon-o(1)
\tag{SE.22}
\]

or

\[
\frac{F_5^{full}}D
\ge {M_*\over15}-\varepsilon-o(1).
\tag{SE.23}
\]

At `epsilon=0`, the common coefficient is

\[
\boxed{
{M_*\over15}=0.254977681830389\ldots .
}
\tag{SE.24}
\]

Every one of these pentagons is source-empty by the existing isolate-host theorem,
since a bounded positive-contact face containing a source point has degree at least
seven.

---

## 5. The flower branch is very far from the rhombic Mantel endpoint

In the flower alternative, (SE.22) fails.  Since

\[
\rho_{\rm rec}=2S,
\qquad
2\tau_D=\rho_{\rm rec}+\sigma_R,
\]

(SE.3) yields

\[
\liminf\frac{\sigma_R}{D}
\ge
2\tau_*-2\left({M_*\over15}-\varepsilon\right).
\tag{SE.25}
\]

In particular, uniformly for `epsilon>=0`, this is at least

\[
\boxed{
\sigma_*:=2\tau_*-{2M_*\over15}
=2.647051289929016\ldots .
}
\tag{SE.26}
\]

The exact strict-rhombus train identity

\[
{T_R^2\over4}-R_4
={\sigma_R\sqrt{R_4}\over2}+{\sigma_R^2\over16}
\tag{SE.27}
\]

and `sqrt(R_4)/D->s_2` therefore give

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T_R^2/4-R_4\over D^2}
\ge
{\sigma_*s_2\over2}+{\sigma_*^2\over16}
=1.698340455132259\ldots .
}
\tag{SE.28}
\]

A fully rhombic pentagon supplies five terminal rhombic-train occurrences, and one
complete train has two terminal occurrences.  Hence the number `N_full` of distinct
non-singleton trains ending on at least one fully rhombic pentagon satisfies

\[
N_{full}\ge{5\over2}F_5^{full}.
\tag{SE.29}
\]

At the exact endpoint, the flower branch therefore gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {N_{full}\over D}
\ge {M_*\over6}
=0.637444204575972\ldots .
}
\tag{SE.30}
\]

This is substantially stronger than the earlier bounded-`t` flower normal form because
terminal surplus saturation simultaneously kills outer detour and superpentagonal
degree excess.

---

## 6. What this resolves and what it does not

The surplus floor itself is not a C-II contradiction.  There is no known universal
upper bound on `mu_D` below `mu_*`.

The useful new information is the stability package.  Any terminal FREE sequence
attempting to keep the canonical all-phase surplus near its smallest value must
converge to a much narrower source-level normal form:

```text
outer detour / D -> 0;
nonunit force-hull-side currency / D -> 0;
(Q-f)/D -> 0;
tau_D/D -> pi/2+g;
f/D, Q/D, P5/D -> 0.76493304549...;
and either
  S/D >= 0.25497768183...,
or
  F5_full/D >= 0.25497768183...,
  N_full/D >= 0.63744420458...,
  (T_R^2/4-R4)/D^2 >= 1.69834045513....
```

The next closing attack should therefore split by this normal form rather than re-run
the relaxed classwise `lambda`/`E_exc` optimization.  In the singleton branch, the
remaining source-level problem is a simultaneous finite motion for a linear family of
one-edge stopped trains.  In the flower branch, the remaining problem is the endpoint
network of a linear family of empty five-rhombus holes together with the very large
rhombic crossing deficit.  The known pentagon phase regression prevents claiming a
uniform angular defect per flower without an additional metric or global-network
input.

No theorem-state file is changed.
